ctions of $n$ real variables $x=(x_1,\\\\dots,x_n)$ with continuous second-order partial derivatives everywhere on $\\\\mathbb{R}^n$. Suppose further that there are constants $c_{ij}$ such that $\\\\frac{\\\\partial f_i}{\\\\partial x_j}-\\\\frac{\\\\partial f_j}{\\\\partial x_i}=c_{ij}$ for all $i$ and $j$, $1 \\\\leq i \\\\leq n$, $1 \\\\leq j \\\\leq n$. Prove that there is a function $g(x)$ on $\\\\mathbb{R}^n$ such that $f_i+\\\\partial g/\\\\partial x_i$ is linear for all $i$, $1 \\\\leq i \\\\leq n$. (A linear function is one of the form $a_0+a_1x_1+a_2x_2+\\\\dots+a_nx_n$.)\\n-/\", \"prechecks\": {\"status\": \"completed\", \"hints\": [{\"kind\": \"natural_arithmetic_review\", \"status\": \"advisory\"}, {\"kind\": \"finite_index_review\", \"status\": \"advisory\"}], \"vacuity\": \"unresolved\"}, \"advisory\": {\"paraphrase_status\": \"truncated\", \"differences\": []}}"
